    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i'm studying for my circuits final and im kinda stuck on capacitors. can u explain the main difference between how they act in a dc circuit vs an ac circuit? also i need the formula for capacitive reactance to make sure i got it right. thanks",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In DC circuits, capacitors predominantly store energy due to voltage, while in AC circuits, they store energy due to the changing current. In AC circuits, capacitors exhibit inductive behavior, opposing changes in current and causing the voltage across the capacitor to lag behind the applied voltage. This is known as capacitive reactance (Xc), which is calculated using the formula: Xc = 1 / (2 * π * f * C), where f is the frequency and C is the capacitance.",
