    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for that. i think i get the math part but i'm a bit confused about the \"opposing changes\" part. like, why does the capacitor actually oppose the current in AC? is there something happening with the charge movement that explains why it can't just let the current flow freely like a resistor would?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When a capacitor charges in an AC circuit, the charge builds up primarily for half of the cycle, causing a \"blank spot\" in the current flow. During the other half of the cycle, the capacitor discharges, allowing current to flow. Effectively, the capacitor prevents the current from building up instantaneously, which would be the case if it only had a voltage source. This opposition to change due to the increasing (+) and decreasing (-) charge on the capacitor plates explains why capacitors behave like inductors in AC circuits, opposing changes in current.",
